WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:08.400
One, two, three, four and five are the five digits given to us.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:10.740
And we have been asked this.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:15.520
How many five digit numbers can be formed using these five digits?

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:18.760
And we need to figure this out for two cases.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:20.880
When repetition is not allowed,

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:25.080
and when repetition is allowed. If you haven't already,

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:27.440
I strongly recommend that you go through

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:30.720
our previous sessions based on forming numbers.

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:34.380
It will help you understand the session much better.

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:36.440
Okay, let's understand

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:39.360
what's been asked. In the first case,

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:41.920
we have been asked to find the number of ways

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:44.260
in which five digit numbers can be formed

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:47.000
when repetition is not allowed.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:52.540
Okay, first things first, let's make five slots for the five digits.

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:56.160
Is there any condition given? No there isn't.

00:00:56.420 --> 00:00:58.920
We have been asked for all five digit numbers

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:01.920
that can be formed using these five digits.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:06.080
As there is no condition we can start from any end.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:08.460
Let's start with the first slot.

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:11.800
In how many ways can the first slot be filled?

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:15.520
We can fill it with any one of the five digits, correct?

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:19.560
Yes, so there are five ways in which it can be filled.

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:24.740
Either a 1 or a 2 or a 3 or a 4 or a 5.

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:28.800
Now assume we have filled up the first slot with one of these digits.

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:33.760
With the first slot being filled, in how many ways can the second slot be filled?

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:37.340
As one digit is used here, four remain!

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:40.300
The second slot can be filled in four ways.

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:42.780
If the first slot is filled with a four,

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:46.800
then the second one can be filled with one, two, three or five.

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:49.260
If the first slot is filled with a two,

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:53.700
then the second one can be filled with a one, three, four or a five.

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:57.160
Now as two digits have been used in the first two slots.

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:00.060
the third one can be filled in three ways.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:05.800
Similarly, the fourth one in two ways, and the last one is just one way.

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:09.380
Do we multiply these? Or do we add them?

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:16.600
Don't forget the basic rules of counting. "Or" is addition "AND" is multiplication.

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:21.120
As we are filling the first and the second and the third slots, and so on...

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:23.620
we will be multiplying these.

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:25.860
Five times four is 20.

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:28.220
20 times 3 is 60.

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:32.160
And 60 times 2 is 120.

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:36.220
There are 125 digit numbers that can be formed

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:41.140
using these five digits provided repetition is not allowed.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:43.900
This is actually 5 factorial.

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:46.480
Many students memorise this.

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:50.820
Five distinct things can be arranged in five factorial ways.

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:53.080
Don't make that mistake.

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:57.880
Yes, you may use 5 factorial directly provided you understand

00:02:57.940 --> 00:02:59.920
why we use the factorial.

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:02.900
Here, we saw it step by step.

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:07.020
why 5, why 4 and why we multiplied them?

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:10.060
Don't memorise the factorial formula.

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:13.900
Understand the logic behind it and then use it.

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:16.280
Now let's move on to the second part.

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:20.680
Five digit numbers when repetition of digits is allowed.

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:23.040
So we are allowed to form numbers like,

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:29.100
11111 or 52333 or 31443

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:31.320
where digits are repeated.

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:35.740
How do we begin? As always we go slot by slot.

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:39.620
The first slot can be filled with any one of the five digits.

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:42.440
It can be filled in five ways.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:45.540
Now I want you to answer the next question.

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:48.840
Having filled the first slot with any one of the digits,

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:52.120
in how many ways can the second slot be filled?

00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:01.700
As repetition is allowed,

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:05.040
it doesn't matter which digit we have filled in the first slot .

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:08.860
The second slot can be filled in five ways again.

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:11.500
Why was it 4 in the previous case?

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:16.000
Because we cannot reuse the digit that was used in the first slot.

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:19.640
In a similar way the third, fourth and the fifth slots

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:22.540
can also be filled in five ways each.

00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:25.740
And we multiply these as we are filling the first,

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:29.620
and the second and the third slots and so on...

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:33.360
We get the answer as five raised to five ways.

00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:37.940
We can form these many five digit numbers using these five digits,

00:04:38.140 --> 00:04:41.240
provided repetition is allowed.

00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:43.040
These problems are easy,

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:46.400
provided you understand the logic of repetition.

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:49.100
Let's look at one last question.

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:53.840
In how many can 6 seats be allocated to 
six people?

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:58.820
Assume that the six people are a, b, c, d, e and f.

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:01.100
And imagine six chairs in a row

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:03.920
where these six people will be seated.

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:06.000
How will you solve this?

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:11.700
In how many ways can these six people be seated on these six chairs?

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:14.680
Pause the video and try solving it.

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:23.640
Think logically!

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:27.900
Isn't this the same as having six different digits and being asked

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:30.700
how many six digit numbers can be formed?

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:33.780
Of course without repetition.

00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:36.320
Six different people are analogous

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:38.360
to six different digits.

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:40.740
Each number formed will be analogous

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:42.740
to each arrangement formed.

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:45.620
And it is obviously without repetition

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:50.000
as one person cannot be seated on two or more seats.

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:52.780
If one person is seated on chair 1,

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:55.960
he cannot be seated on Chair 2 as well, Right?

00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:58.380
And what will the answer be?

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:00.880
Let's go back to the previous page.

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:06.420
Five digits in five slots when repetition is not allowed is this case.

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:07.980
5 Factorial.

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:12.920
Going back to the current problem the answer to it will be 6 factorial.

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:18.280
6 people can be arranged on 6 seats, in 6 factorial ways.

00:06:18.580 --> 00:06:21.740
Here we have assumed that the seats are in a row.

00:06:21.980 --> 00:06:26.800
The answer will be different if the seats are arranged in a circular fashion.

00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:28.940
Don't worry about it for now!

00:06:29.340 --> 00:06:32.780
We have covered circular arrangements in another session.

